===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[coddle]]; to [[pamper]] somebody like an infant.
#* {{RQ:Lewis Babbitt|passage={{...}} though he tried to be gruff and mature, he yielded to her and was glad to be '''babied'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1944|author=w:Emily Carr|title=w:The House of All Sorts|chapter=Friction|pageurl=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks01/0100121h.html
|passage=Then the man effected measles and stayed off the job for six weeks, '''babying''' himself at home, though he lived just round the corner from my half-built house.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[tend]] (something) with [[care]]; to be [[overly]] [[attentive]] to (something), [[fuss over]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Mr. Mac and His Team|journal=[[w:Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=31 March 1967|titleurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130721111413/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,941104,00.html
|passage=In the past 27 years, "Mr. Mac," as he is known to his 46,000 teammates, has built and '''babied''' his McDonnell Co. from nothing into a $1 billion-a-year corporation.}}
#* '''1912''', Linda Craig, interviewed by Theresa Forte, "Tree and Twig farm — a treasure chest of heirloom tomatoes," ''Welland Tribune'', 25 May, 2012, [https://web.archive.org/web/20171205052150/http://www.wellandtribune.ca/2012/05/23/tree-and-twig-farm--a-treasure-chest-of-heirloom-tomatoes]
#*: I have grown them for years and although some years are better than others, I have always had loads of tomatoes by not '''babying''' them, going easy on the water, and fertilizing with compost in the planting hole.

